INSERT INTO `week_learn` (`id`, `week_n`, `ord`, `text`) VALUES
  (1, 1, 1, 'javascript.info chapters 4, 5 and 6. Objects, object references and copying, garbage collection, methods and \"this\", constructors, optional chaining, symbols, object-to-primitive conversion, data types in full, then functions: recursion, rest and spread, closures, the old \"var\", the global object, function objects and NFE, the \"new Function\" syntax, scheduling, decorators and forwarding, call/apply/bind, arrow functions revisited.'),
  (2, 1, 2, 'Pro Git chapters 2 and 3 in full. Then learngitbranching.js.org until the animations bore you.'),
  (3, 1, 3, 'Branching, merging, rebase, interactive rebase, cherry-pick, bisect, reflog, stash, and how to undo every one of those.'),
  (4, 1, 4, 'The Missing Semester 2026 lectures: Course Overview and the Shell, Command-line Environment, Version Control and Git.'),
  (5, 2, 1, 'Semantic HTML, the box model, cascade and specificity, custom properties, container queries, logical properties.'),
  (6, 2, 2, 'Flexbox Froggy, all 24 levels. Grid Garden, all 28 levels. Then rebuild both layouts from empty without the game.'),
  (7, 2, 3, 'Responsive layout with media queries written by hand. No Tailwind, no component library, nothing.'),
  (8, 2, 4, 'web.dev Learn CSS for the parts the games do not cover: stacking contexts, z-index, overflow, inheritance.'),
  (9, 2, 5, 'Linux and networking gap-fill: permissions and the octal you keep looking up, processes and signals, ports and sockets, DNS resolution order, TCP against UDP, what nginx is actually doing as a reverse proxy.'),
  (10, 3, 1, 'javascript.info chapters 7, 8, 9 and 10. Prototypes and prototypal inheritance, classes and class inheritance, static properties, private and protected, extending built-ins, instanceof, mixins, error handling and custom errors.'),
  (11, 3, 2, 'HTTP properly: request methods and when each is idempotent, the status codes that matter and what 301 against 302 actually changes, headers, content negotiation, cookies and their attributes, caching (Cache-Control, ETag, Last-Modified, stale-while-revalidate), and CORS from first principles including preflight.'),
  (12, 3, 3, 'Why CORS exists at all. If you cannot explain the same-origin policy you will copy and paste your way past it forever.'),
  (13, 4, 1, 'javascript.info chapter 11 in full: callbacks, promises, promise chaining, error handling with promises, the Promise API (all, allSettled, race, any), promisification, microtasks, async/await. Give this two full days.'),
  (14, 4, 2, 'javascript.info chapter 12: generators and async iteration.'),
  (15, 4, 3, 'The event loop, visualised. latentflip.com/loupe once, then jsv9000.app until the microtask queue is obvious.'),
  (16, 4, 4, 'Write a retry with exponential backoff and jitter by hand. No library. Then an AbortController-based timeout. Then a concurrency limiter.'),
  (17, 4, 5, 'Async is the single largest source of bugs you will write this year and the single most common live-coding question for Node roles.'),
  (18, 5, 1, 'Full Stack Open parts 0, 1 and 2. Fundamentals of web apps, introduction to React, component state and event handlers, rendering collections, forms, getting and altering data on a server, styling.'),
  (19, 5, 2, 'react.dev Learn alongside it. It is the current source of truth and Full Stack Open is not always in step with it.'),
  (20, 5, 3, 'The rules of hooks, why they exist, and what actually breaks when you violate them.'),
  (21, 5, 4, 'react.dev Learn: You Might Not Need an Effect. Read it twice. Most junior React code is effects that should not exist.'),
  (22, 5, 5, 'React 19.2 is current and React Compiler 1.0 shipped in October 2025. Most useMemo and useCallback advice in older courses is obsolete. Do not write memo by reflex.'),
  (23, 6, 1, 'Custom hooks, context, when to lift state and when not to, composition over configuration, keys and reconciliation, controlled against uncontrolled inputs.'),
  (24, 6, 2, 'react.dev Learn: Escape Hatches in full. Refs, effects, effect lifecycle, removing effect dependencies, custom hooks.'),
  (25, 6, 3, 'react.dev Learn: React Compiler. Understand what it now handles so you stop hand-memoising.'),
  (26, 6, 4, 'Tailwind CSS v4 with CSS-first configuration. There is no tailwind.config.js by default any more; theme tokens live in an @theme block in your CSS. A v3 tutorial will have you writing a config file that is silently ignored.'),
  (27, 6, 5, 'Tailwind v4 specifics: @theme, @utility, @variant, the Vite plugin, and how the Lightning CSS engine changes build setup.'),
  (28, 7, 1, 'The TypeScript Handbook start to finish: basic types, narrowing, functions, objects, type manipulation (generics, keyof, typeof, indexed access, conditional types, mapped types, template literal types), classes, modules.'),
  (29, 7, 2, 'Total TypeScript free tutorials for the parts that will not stick, especially generics and narrowing.'),
  (30, 7, 3, 'Type Challenges, easy tier only. Do not rabbit-hole into medium.'),
  (31, 7, 4, 'tsconfig: strict, noUncheckedIndexedAccess, exactOptionalPropertyTypes, verbatimModuleSyntax. Turn them on at file one and never turn them off.'),
  (32, 7, 5, 'Typing React properly: component props, children, generics in components, event handler types, useRef types, discriminated unions for state.'),
  (33, 8, 1, 'Node.js Learn path: modules, the event loop in Node specifically, timers and nextTick, streams, the file system, worker threads at a conceptual level.'),
  (34, 8, 2, 'Express: routing, middleware order, error-handling middleware, router composition.'),
  (35, 8, 3, 'API design: resource naming, HTTP semantics, pagination (cursor over offset and why), filtering, sorting, versioning strategy, rate limiting, idempotency keys, consistent error envelopes with a machine-readable code.'),
  (36, 8, 4, 'Validate every input at the boundary with a schema library (zod or valibot). Every single one. Parse, do not validate.'),
  (37, 8, 5, 'The Node Best Practices repository. Read the whole thing once, then bookmark the sections you disagreed with.'),
  (38, 9, 1, 'Schema design: normalisation to third normal form and when to deliberately break it, natural against surrogate keys, constraints as documentation, check constraints, foreign keys and ON DELETE behaviour.'),
  (39, 9, 2, 'PostgreSQL Exercises. Every category: basic, joins and subqueries, modifying data, aggregation, timestamps, string operations, recursive queries. Roughly 80 exercises. Do all of them.'),
  (40, 9, 3, 'Indexes: B-tree, composite index column order, covering indexes, partial indexes, expression indexes, and why an index on a low-cardinality column is usually a waste.'),
  (41, 9, 4, 'Transactions and isolation levels: read committed, repeatable read, serializable. Know what a dirty read, a non-repeatable read and a phantom read are, and which PostgreSQL actually allows.'),
  (42, 9, 5, 'Migrations. Pick Prisma or Drizzle and stay. Migrations live in the repository and run in CI.'),
  (43, 9, 6, 'PostgreSQL 18 specifics: asynchronous I/O, skip scan on multicolumn B-trees, uuidv7() for timestamp-ordered keys, virtual generated columns as the new default.'),
  (44, 10, 1, 'nextjs.org/learn end to end, then read the caching documentation twice.'),
  (45, 10, 2, 'App Router: layouts, templates, loading and error boundaries, route groups, parallel and intercepting routes.'),
  (46, 10, 3, 'Server Components against Client Components. The boundary, serialisation rules, and why \"use client\" is a leaf-ward directive.'),
  (47, 10, 4, 'Server Actions, route handlers, streaming and Suspense boundaries.'),
  (48, 10, 5, 'Next.js 16 specifics you will not find in a v15 tutorial: Turbopack is the default bundler for dev and production; the middleware file is renamed to proxy and the edge runtime is not supported there; Partial Prerendering is opted into through the cacheComponents configuration flag rather than experimental.ppr.'),
  (49, 10, 6, 'Next.js 15 leaves Maintenance LTS on 21 October 2026, which falls inside this roadmap. Build on 16.'),
  (50, 11, 1, 'Pilcrow’s Auth Book at auth.pilcrowonpaper.com, cover to cover. This replaced The Copenhagen Book, which is archived. Read it twice; it is short and it is the highest-value free resource on this list.'),
  (51, 11, 2, 'Sessions: generate a high-entropy token, store only its SHA-256 hash in the database, set the cookie HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, with a sensible Max-Age and a rolling refresh.'),
  (52, 11, 3, 'Password storage per the OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet: Argon2id at m=19456 (19 MiB), t=2, p=1. That exact configuration, verified against the cheat sheet.'),
  (53, 11, 4, 'Email verification, password reset tokens with single use and short expiry, rate limiting on every auth endpoint, timing-safe comparison, and generic error messages that do not enumerate accounts.'),
  (54, 11, 5, 'OAuth if time allows: authorisation code flow with PKCE, state parameter, and account linking rules.'),
  (55, 11, 6, 'WebSockets: one live feature a stranger can see working. Presence or notifications. Handle reconnection and backpressure.'),
  (56, 12, 1, 'Vitest: unit tests where they matter, not everywhere. Test the matching rules, the money arithmetic, the date handling and the auth logic. Do not test framework behaviour.'),
  (57, 12, 2, 'Testing Library: query by role and by accessible name. Test what the user sees, not implementation detail.'),
  (58, 12, 3, 'Mock Service Worker: fake the network at the network boundary, not by monkey-patching your own modules.'),
  (59, 12, 4, 'One integration test that hits a real PostgreSQL in a container, seeded and torn down per run. This is the test that catches the bugs unit tests never will.'),
  (60, 12, 5, 'Playwright: two end-to-end journeys only. Login and the core happy path. Read the best-practices page before writing them.'),
  (61, 12, 6, 'GitHub Actions: run lint, typecheck, unit, integration and e2e on every push. Block merge on red. Cache dependencies. Secrets in encrypted secrets and environment variables, never in git, not once.'),
  (62, 12, 7, 'Kent C. Dodds, Write Tests. Not Too Many. Mostly Integration. Read it and take the ratio seriously.'),
  (63, 13, 1, 'OWASP Top 10:2025, the eighth installment, in the current order: A01 Broken Access Control, A02 Security Misconfiguration, A03 Software Supply Chain Failures, A04 Cryptographic Failures, A05 Injection, A06 Insecure Design, A07 Authentication Failures, A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures, A09 Security Logging and Alerting Failures, A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions.'),
  (64, 13, 2, 'A03 Software Supply Chain Failures is new in this position and it is broader than the old \"vulnerable components\" category. It covers build tools, pipelines, containers and package registries. A10 is entirely new and replaced Server-Side Request Forgery.'),
  (65, 13, 3, 'Most tutorials still link the 2021 list. Anyone quoting A10 as SSRF is working from stale material.'),
  (66, 13, 4, 'OWASP Cheat Sheet Series for the parts that touch your stack: Authorization, Input Validation, SQL Injection Prevention, Cross-Site Request Forgery, Content Security Policy, Secrets Management, Logging.'),
  (67, 13, 5, 'PortSwigger Web Security Academy: access control labs and SQL injection labs. Free, and better than any paid course.'),
  (68, 13, 6, 'Dependency hygiene: lockfile committed, npm audit or an equivalent in CI, Dependabot or Renovate, provenance and pinned actions in your GitHub workflows.'),
  (69, 14, 1, 'Docker: images against containers, layers and cache invalidation, multi-stage builds, distroless or slim base images, non-root users, healthchecks, .dockerignore.'),
  (70, 14, 2, 'Docker Compose v2. Note the space, not a hyphen; the hyphenated v1 command is dead and you will get \"command not found\".'),
  (71, 14, 3, 'Get the production image small and explain why each layer exists.'),
  (72, 14, 4, 'Deploy to your own Oracle Cloud box, not to a platform that hides everything. You already run this server; this week you do it deliberately instead of by copy and paste.'),
  (73, 14, 5, 'Caddy or nginx in front, automatic TLS, and a rollback you have actually tested.'),
  (74, 14, 6, 'Redis or Valkey: caching with sensible TTLs, cache invalidation strategy, session storage, sliding-window rate limits, and the cache stampede problem.'),
  (75, 14, 7, 'Licensing, because it comes up: since Redis 8.0 in May 2025 Redis Open Source is tri-licensed under RSALv2, SSPLv1 and AGPLv3. Valkey is the Linux Foundation fork of Redis 7.2.4 and remains BSD-3-Clause. Know the difference and have a one-sentence opinion.'),
  (76, 15, 1, 'BullMQ on Redis: producers, workers, concurrency, rate limiting, delayed and repeatable jobs, retries with backoff, dead letter handling, and idempotent handlers so a retry cannot double-process.'),
  (77, 15, 2, 'System Design Primer, the first third only. Do not read all of it. The first third is the part you are actually asked about.'),
  (78, 15, 3, 'Draw one architecture diagram of your own application, by hand, and be able to redraw it live in an interview in under three minutes.'),
  (79, 15, 4, 'Performance: find the slowest query in Project 3, run EXPLAIN ANALYZE, read the plan at explain.dalibo.com, add the right index, run it again. Write both numbers in the README in milliseconds, not adjectives.'),
  (80, 15, 5, 'Structured logging with Pino: JSON, request IDs, correlation IDs, log levels that mean something, and never console.log in production paths.'),
  (81, 15, 6, 'One metric that matters plus an error tracker. Sentry for errors, and either SigNoz or OpenTelemetry for traces.'),
  (82, 15, 7, 'Gate 3 also opens the application phase. From this week the learning block drops from three hours to two, and the hour moves to applications. That trade is not optional.'),
  (83, 16, 1, 'AI SDK 7, released June 2026. Two hard constraints to know before you install: all packages are now ESM-only, so require() will not work, and the minimum Node version is 22. You are on Node 24, so you are fine.'),
  (84, 16, 2, 'If you follow any tutorial written against AI SDK 6 or earlier, run the codemod rather than hand-porting: npx @ai-sdk/codemod v7.'),
  (85, 16, 3, 'Core surface: generateText, streamText, generateObject, streamObject, tool definitions, the tool loop, tool approval, and provider-agnostic model strings.'),
  (86, 16, 4, 'Tokens, context windows, temperature, top-p, stop sequences. What each one actually changes.'),
  (87, 16, 5, 'Cost mechanics: input tokens against output tokens, prompt caching, embedding cost at ingest against inference cost at query time. Applied AI interviews ask about cost and almost no junior candidate has an answer.'),
  (88, 16, 6, 'Structured output with a schema, and what to do when the model returns something that does not validate.'),
  (89, 16, 7, 'You are building on models, not making them. Fine-tuning is not on this roadmap and that is deliberate.'),
  (90, 17, 1, 'pgvector: your vector store is just PostgreSQL. Indexing limits matter. The vector type indexes up to 2,000 dimensions, halfvec up to 4,000, bit up to 64,000 and sparsevec up to 1,000 non-zero elements. Raw storage goes to 16,000 dimensions but only the indexable ceiling counts for search.'),
  (91, 17, 2, 'Practical consequence: a 3,072-dimension embedding will not build an index as vector. Use halfvec. That one line saves you a full day.'),
  (92, 17, 3, 'HNSW against IVFFlat: build time, recall, memory, and when each is the right answer.'),
  (93, 17, 4, 'Chunking: fixed against semantic against structural. For scanned tender PDFs, structural chunking on headings and clause numbers beats naive character splits every time.'),
  (94, 17, 5, 'Anthropic Contextual Retrieval, September 2024, measured on their own benchmark: contextual embeddings alone cut top-20 retrieval failure from 5.7 per cent to 3.7 per cent, a 35 per cent reduction; adding contextual BM25 took it to 2.9 per cent, a 49 per cent reduction; adding reranking took it to 1.9 per cent, a 67 per cent reduction. Implement all three stages.'),
  (95, 17, 6, 'Hybrid retrieval: run dense and BM25 at once, then fuse. Reciprocal rank fusion is the simple default.'),
  (96, 17, 7, 'Reranking: cut to top-k with a cross-encoder or a hosted reranker before generation.'),
  (97, 17, 8, 'Every answer cites the exact page it came from. No exceptions. This is the single feature that separates your project from a chatbot.'),
  (98, 18, 1, 'Ragas metrics for retrieval-augmented generation: Faithfulness, Response Relevancy, Context Precision, Context Recall, Context Entities Recall, Noise Sensitivity.'),
  (99, 18, 2, 'Faithfulness is the number of claims in the response supported by the retrieved context divided by the total number of claims in the response. Know that definition cold; it is asked directly.'),
  (100, 18, 3, 'Context Precision is the mean of precision-at-k across the retrieved chunks, which rewards putting relevant chunks near the top of the ranking.'),
  (101, 18, 4, 'Build a fixed evaluation set of at least fifty question-and-answer pairs from real tender documents. Freeze it. An eval set you change when the score is bad is not an eval set.'),
  (102, 18, 5, 'Ragas is a Python library with no JavaScript port. This is why Python sits on Working Sundays 1 to 3 and not after the roadmap.'),
  (103, 18, 6, 'Prompt injection and LLM-specific risk from the OWASP GenAI Security Project. Your retrieval corpus is an attack surface: a poisoned document can carry instructions to your model.'),
  (104, 18, 7, 'Defences: treat retrieved text as data and never as instructions, constrain tool permissions, require citations, and refuse when nothing scores above threshold.'),
  (105, 18, 8, 'Put the eval numbers in the README. Numbers, not claims. Then write the honest failure modes underneath them.'),
  (106, 19, 1, 'The agent loop written by hand: plan, act, observe, repeat, with a termination condition and a step budget. No framework. You must be able to draw this on a whiteboard.'),
  (107, 19, 2, 'Tool design: narrow tools with tight schemas beat one tool that does everything. Errors returned to the model must be actionable.'),
  (108, 19, 3, 'Anthropic, Building Effective Agents: the distinction between workflows and agents, and the standard patterns (prompt chaining, routing, parallelisation, orchestrator-workers, evaluator-optimiser).'),
  (109, 19, 4, 'Model Context Protocol. Critical currency point: the current specification revision is 2026-07-28 and it made MCP stateless. Protocol-level sessions and the Mcp-Session-Id header were removed from the Streamable HTTP transport, and the initialize / notifications-initialized handshake was removed; every request now carries its protocol version and client capabilities in _meta. Servers needing cross-call state use explicit server-minted handles passed as ordinary tool arguments.'),
  (110, 19, 5, 'Anything you read about MCP written before mid-2026 describes a stateful protocol that no longer exists. Build to 2026-07-28.'),
  (111, 19, 6, 'Build one small MCP server with three to five well-scoped tools over your Tender Fit corpus. Test it with MCP Inspector.'),
  (112, 19, 7, 'Only after that, open a framework, so you know exactly what it is hiding.'),
  (113, 20, 1, 'Ten mock interviews. Exponent Practice for peer mocks; Pramp moved to Exponent Practice in July 2024 and any link to Pramp now redirects there. interviewing.io for anonymous mocks with real engineers.'),
  (114, 20, 2, 'Four of the ten are case studies, not coding mocks. Same ten sessions, four of them changed in shape.'),
  (115, 20, 3, 'System design out loud, to a wall if you have to. Record it. Watch it back once.'),
  (116, 20, 4, 'Reread failed-twice.md from the first line. Every problem in it, again, timed.'),
  (117, 20, 5, 'DSA this week is revision and timed sets only. New problems feel better and help less now.'),
  (118, 20, 6, 'Rebuild the resume now, not earlier, because only now do the numbers exist. One page, four projects, three variants.'),
  (119, 20, 7, 'Beyond Cracking the Coding Interview, the free chapters, for the behavioural and negotiation structure.'),
  (120, 21, 1, 'Apply to the role name, not to companies you have heard of. Search Applied AI Engineer, Forward Deployed Engineer, Solutions Engineer. Not \"software jobs\".'),
  (121, 21, 2, 'Apply inside twenty-four hours of a posting. Position in the pile is real and it decays fast.'),
  (122, 21, 3, 'Referral asks every single time. The commonly repeated industry figure is that referrals are roughly seven per cent of applications and around forty per cent of hires; treat the exact numbers as directional, but the direction is not in dispute. The worst answer is no.'),
  (123, 21, 4, 'Wellfound filtered to companies with one to fifty employees. They read every application. Large companies filter you out on the degree line before a human sees it.'),
  (124, 21, 5, 'LinkedIn Easy Apply on posts under twenty-four hours old, plus one polite direct message with your live link and a one-line ask.'),
  (125, 21, 6, 'Track every application in a sheet: company, role, date, channel, referral yes or no, response, stage. You cannot fix a funnel you are not measuring.'),
  (126, 21, 7, 'levels.fyi and AmbitionBox before any salary conversation, so the first number you hear is not the first number you have thought about.')
INSERT INTO `week_build` (`id`, `week_n`, `ord`, `text`) VALUES
  (1, 1, 1, 'Repository hygiene: .gitignore, .editorconfig, conventional commit messages, a README that is not the default, and a LICENSE.'),
  (2, 1, 2, 'One small utility written from empty and pushed with a clean, readable history of at least fifteen commits.'),
  (3, 2, 1, 'A static site you wrote every line of, responsive, live on the internet on a real domain or subdomain.'),
  (4, 3, 1, 'Project 1 (ITC Reclaim) repository created. Problem statement, data model sketch, and first commits in.'),
  (5, 4, 1, 'Project 1: async file ingest, streaming parse, progress reporting.'),
  (6, 5, 1, 'Project 1 front end in React. Upload two files, get a four-bucket mismatch report.'),
  (7, 6, 1, 'Project 1 styled, responsive, accessible, and still deployed.'),
  (8, 7, 1, 'Project 1 converted end to end. Every file. Zero uses of any. Still deployed, still working.'),
  (9, 8, 1, 'Project 2 API: the first live endpoint set, with a POST that rejects bad input properly.'),
  (10, 9, 1, 'Project 2 schema designed, migrated, seeded, with indexes you can justify.'),
  (11, 10, 1, 'Project 2 front end on Next.js 16, deployed early and deployed often.'),
  (12, 11, 1, 'Project 2 with authentication you wrote by hand, plus one WebSocket feature, both live.'),
  (13, 12, 1, 'Project 3 begins: same product as Project 2, made operable. This week it gets a test suite and CI.'),
  (14, 13, 1, 'Project 3: a written threat model, plus the fixes for whatever it finds.'),
  (15, 14, 1, 'Project 3 fully containerised, running on your own server, with Redis or Valkey behind it.'),
  (16, 15, 1, 'Project 3 shipped and operable. Queue, indexes, logs, metrics, error tracking, and the number in the README.'),
  (17, 16, 1, 'Project 4 (Tender Fit) begins. Ingest pipeline and the first streamed answer.'),
  (18, 17, 1, 'Project 4: full retrieval pipeline with citations, deployed.'),
  (19, 18, 1, 'Project 4 with published eval numbers and a written failure-mode section.'),
  (20, 19, 1, 'Project 4 deployed with the hand-written agent loop and a live MCP server.'),
  (21, 20, 1, 'No new build. Polish: READMEs, screenshots, demo videos, live-link health checks on all four projects.'),
  (22, 21, 1, 'Nothing new is built. Every hour that is not applications goes to keeping the four live links healthy.')
INSERT INTO `week_ships` (`id`, `week_n`, `ord`, `text`) VALUES
  (1, 1, 1, 'Your first public repository with a real README and a commit history a stranger can read.'),
  (2, 2, 1, 'A static site, live, that you wrote every line of, served by nginx on your own box.'),
  (3, 3, 1, 'Project 1 repository public, problem statement written, skeleton deployed.'),
  (4, 4, 1, 'Project 1 ingesting real files asynchronously, deployed, commits every day.'),
  (5, 5, 1, 'GATE 1. Project 1 on a public URL a stranger can open and use.'),
  (6, 6, 1, 'Project 1 styled, responsive, accessible, still live.'),
  (7, 7, 1, 'Project 1 fully in TypeScript, strict mode, still deployed, still working.'),
  (8, 8, 1, 'Your first real API, live, with at least one POST that rejects malformed input with a useful error.'),
  (9, 9, 1, 'A schema you designed yourself, with migrations in the repository and every index justified in writing.'),
  (10, 10, 1, 'Project 2 front end live on Next.js 16, deployed at least three times this week.'),
  (11, 11, 1, 'GATE 2. Authentication you wrote yourself, running on the deployed application. One WebSocket feature a stranger can watch work.'),
  (12, 12, 1, 'A green CI badge on the README that means something, and a merge that is genuinely blocked when tests fail.'),
  (13, 13, 1, 'A written threat list for Project 3 in your own words, and the code changes that close the top five.'),
  (14, 14, 1, 'Project 3 containerised end to end, running on your own server, with a rollback you have executed at least once.'),
  (15, 15, 1, 'GATE 3. Project 3 shipped and operable. Applications start going out this week.'),
  (16, 16, 1, 'Project 4 started. One streamed, tool-using answer live on a public URL.'),
  (17, 17, 1, 'Project 4 answering questions with citations to the exact source page, deployed.'),
  (18, 18, 1, 'Real Ragas numbers in the README, plus a failure-mode section you wrote yourself.'),
  (19, 19, 1, 'Project 4 live with a hand-written agent loop and a working MCP server a stranger can connect to.'),
  (20, 20, 1, 'Ten mocks behind you, recorded and watched back. Resume rebuilt with real numbers. All four live links verified.'),
  (21, 21, 1, 'GATE 4. Project 4 live, one hundred applications sent, interviews in the calendar.')
INSERT INTO `week_traps` (`week_n`, `text`) VALUES
  (1, 'Learning only add, commit, push, then panicking the first time you need to undo something in front of an interviewer.'),
  (2, 'Reaching for Tailwind before you can centre a div without it. In Week 6 you will be asked to and it will be too late to learn.'),
  (3, 'Treating CORS as a thing you copy and paste your way past. It is one of the three most common junior interview questions in India.'),
  (4, 'Skimming chapter 11. Everything after this week assumes you understood it, and React, Node and every RAG pipeline are async end to end.'),
  (5, 'Deploying on Sunday night for the first time and discovering the build breaks. Friday deploy exists for exactly this reason.'),
  (6, 'Following a Tailwind v3 tutorial and writing a tailwind.config.js file that does nothing. It will not error. It will just be ignored.'),
  (7, 'Sprinkling any to make errors go away. You are then writing JavaScript with extra steps and a false sense of safety.'),
  (8, 'No validation, then a crash during a live demo. It happens to everyone once. Make it happen in Week 8 and not in an interview.'),
  (9, 'Skipping indexes until it is already slow, then guessing which one to add. Guessing is visible in an interview.'),
  (10, 'Following a v15 tutorial without noticing. The caching model and the middleware file both changed, and you will chase ghosts for a day.'),
  (11, 'Reaching for a library and learning nothing. If you cannot explain how your session survives a server restart, you did not build it.'),
  (12, 'Testing everything and shipping nothing. Twelve good tests beat sixty generated ones, and an interviewer can tell the difference in thirty seconds.'),
  (13, 'Studying the 2021 list because it is what most tutorials still link to. Citing A06:2021 in a 2027 interview marks you as out of date.'),
  (14, 'Learning Kubernetes because a video said so. You do not need it, you will not be asked for it at this level, and it will cost you a week you do not have.'),
  (15, 'Optimising something that was never slow. Measure first, always. An unmeasured optimisation is a story you cannot defend.'),
  (16, 'Going down the fine-tuning rabbit hole. It is not on this roadmap for a reason and it will cost you the Applied AI project.'),
  (17, 'Naive vector search over raw chunks, then wondering why the answers are wrong. It is the most common failure in every junior RAG portfolio.'),
  (18, 'Skipping this week because it is the Christmas and New Year window. This is the week that makes Project 4 credible instead of impressive.'),
  (19, 'Starting with a framework. You will not be able to explain what it does, and \"I used LangChain\" is not an answer to \"how does your agent decide to stop\".'),
  (20, 'Doing new problems instead of revising, and polishing the resume for a week instead of sending it.'),
  (21, 'Polishing the resume for another week instead of sending it. The maths is simple: more applications, more interviews.')
INSERT INTO `week_notes` (`week_n`, `text`) VALUES
  (1, 'Use git bisect once on purpose. Break something, then find it. You will remember it forever.'),
  (2, 'You already run this server. This week connects what you have been doing to why it works. That is a different thing from knowing it.'),
  (3, 'Write the CORS explanation in your own words in the log. If you cannot write it, you do not have it.'),
  (4, 'Two full days on one chapter looks slow. It is the highest-return week in the entire plan.'),
  (5, 'Deployed means a stranger opens the link and it works. Localhost is not shipping. There is no partial credit here.'),
  (6, 'If you must keep a JS config, Tailwind v4 still reads it through @config \"./tailwind.config.js\". Know that this is a compatibility path, not the default.'),
  (7, 'Converting a working app teaches more than starting typed. You see exactly what the types were protecting you from.'),
  (8, 'Node 24 Krypton is the Active LTS line until 20 October 2026. See Part 6 for the Node 26 decision point, which lands inside this roadmap.'),
  (9, 'Move your local PostgreSQL from 16 to 18 this week so you are not learning behaviour that changed underneath you.'),
  (10, 'Write down, in one paragraph, what runs on the server and what ships to the browser. If you cannot, you do not have it yet.'),
  (11, 'At work you would use a library. Say exactly that in the interview, then explain what the library is doing. That answer scores higher than either extreme.'),
  (12, 'Take a screenshot of a red CI run blocking a merge. That screenshot is an interview answer.'),
  (13, 'You already run fail2ban and ufw on the VPS. This week tells you what you were defending against and lets you say it out loud.'),
  (14, 'Record the rollback. Thirty seconds of screen capture. It is the single most convincing artefact a junior candidate can show a backend team.'),
  (15, 'The before-and-after number is a complete interview answer on its own. Most candidates at this level have nothing in that slot.'),
  (16, 'Budget roughly Rs 1,500 of API credit for Weeks 16 to 19. That is the only unavoidable spend in the whole plan.'),
  (17, 'Indian hiring slows from roughly 21 to 27 December. Build through it. The replies land in the first week of January.'),
  (18, 'A candidate with published eval numbers and an honest failure list beats a candidate with two more features. Every time.'),
  (19, 'You have already shipped an MCP server with seven tools. Do it again, properly, against the current specification, and this time you can defend every line.'),
  (20, 'Speaking while you code is a separate skill from coding. It has to be practised, and this is the only week allocated to it.'),
  (21, 'Do not optimise the funnel before you have filled it. One hundred applications is the input; the funnel maths only becomes readable after that.')
